==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{etyl|la|en}} {{lena}} {{m|la|conglomeratus}}, past participle of {{m|la|conglomerare||to roll together}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|noun|adjective}}
** {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/kənˈɡlɒm(ə)ɹət/|lang=en}}
** {{a|US}} {{IPA|/kənˈɡlɑm(ə)ɹət/|lang=en}}
* {{a|verb}}
** {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/kənˈɡlɒməɹeɪt/|lang=en}}
** {{a|US}} {{IPA|/kənˈɡlɑməɹeɪt/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[cluster]] of [[heterogeneous]] things.
#* Trench
#*: A '''conglomerate''' of marvellous anecdotes.
# {{lb|en|business}} A [[corporation]] formed by the combination of several smaller corporations whose activities are unrelated to the corporation's primary activity.
# {{lb|en|geology}} A [[rock]] consisting of [[gravel]] or [[pebble]]s embedded in a [[matrix]].
#* Lyell (1797 – 1875)
#*: A '''conglomerate''', therefore, is simply gravel bound together by a cement.
#* '''1864''': Fitz-Hugh Ludlow in ''The Atlantic'' 
#*: ...natural colossi from two to five hundred feet high, done in argillaceous sandstone or a singular species of '''conglomerate''', all of which owe their existence almost entirely to the agency of wind.

